# The Analyst Agent

The analyst is the first **brain** in this workbook. A brain is not a function you
call once — it is a system prompt, a model, and a set of tools, run as a loop on
the **server** (the nexus). Native Elixir on the BEAM drives the loop: it calls the
model, reads what the model wants to do, runs the tool, feeds the result back, and
repeats until the model says it's done. The browser never sees a key; the loop lives
where the trusted tier lives.

The analyst's job is concrete: take the top-ranked leads off the [[Pipeline]] and
draft outreach for each one. It reads the ranked leads, looks up a little context per
lead, and writes a short, specific first email. It is the pointy end of everything the
earlier files built — [[Enrich]] filled the fields, [[Score]] ranked them, and now the
analyst turns a `%Lead{}` into a sentence a human will actually send.

## Memory is typed data, not a transcript blob

Most agent frameworks treat "memory" as an opaque string that grows forever. Here
memory is a **struct** — the same modelling discipline as the rest of the workbook.
The analyst remembers what it drafted, for whom, and why, as typed values it can
query later. That is what makes the loop auditable: the trace is data on the code
graph, not prose in a log file.

defmodule Draft do
  defstruct lead: nil, subject: "", body: "", angle: :value, sent: false
end

defmodule AnalystMemory do
  defstruct drafts: [], leads_seen: 0, model: "minimax/minimax-m3", notes: []

  def remember(%AnalystMemory{} = mem, %Draft{} = draft) do
    %AnalystMemory{
      mem
      | drafts: [draft | mem.drafts],
        leads_seen: mem.leads_seen + 1
    }
  end

  def for_lead(%AnalystMemory{drafts: drafts}, %Lead{domain: domain}) do
    Enum.find(drafts, fn %Draft{lead: %Lead{domain: d}} -> d == domain end)
  end
end

The `angle` field is a small enum — `:value | :urgency | :referral` — the same
set-of-atoms pattern used for `@statuses`. It records *why* a draft reads the way it
does, so a later review pass (the [[Reflexion]] agent) can critique the choice without
re-deriving it.

## The tools the brain may call

A tool is just a server function the loop is allowed to invoke. The model proposes a
call by name; the loop validates and runs it. The analyst gets three: read the ranked
leads, look up firmographic context, and write a draft into typed memory. Note that
every tool is a normal Elixir function — no special tool DSL, the module *is* the
contract. Its public functions are the exports the brain sees.

defmodule AnalystTools do
  @doc "Pull the top leads off the pipeline, highest score first."
  def top_leads(limit \\ 3) do
    Pipeline.ranked()
    |> Enum.filter(fn %Lead{status: s} -> s == :scored end)
    |> Enum.take(limit)
  end

  @doc "Cheap firmographic context for one lead — used to make the angle specific."
  def context(%Lead{revenue: revenue, employees: employees}) do
    cond do
      revenue >= 75_000 -> :enterprise
      employees <= 10 -> :early_stage
      true -> :growth
    end
  end

  @doc "Compose a draft. The angle is chosen from the lead's shape, not at random."
  def draft(%Lead{name: name} = lead) do
    angle = pick_angle(lead)

    %Draft{
      lead: lead,
      subject: subject_for(name, angle),
      body: body_for(lead, angle),
      angle: angle
    }
  end

  defp pick_angle(%Lead{} = lead) do
    case context(lead) do
      :enterprise -> :value
      :early_stage -> :referral
      :growth -> :urgency
    end
  end

  defp subject_for(name, :value), do: "#{name}: cut sales-cycle time"
  defp subject_for(name, :referral), do: "#{name} — quick intro from a mutual"
  defp subject_for(name, :urgency), do: "#{name}, before your next quarter closes"

  defp body_for(%Lead{name: name, employees: employees}, angle) do
    opener =
      case angle do
        :value -> "Teams your size lose a week per deal to lead triage."
        :referral -> "We work with a few companies in your space."
        :urgency -> "You're scaling fast — #{employees} people and climbing."
      end

    "#{opener} #{name} could rank inbound leads automatically. Worth 15 minutes?"
  end
end

## The brain itself

A brain is a **server definition** — it declares *what the agent is* (prompt, model,
tools), and the nexus supplies *how it runs* (the loop). It is not a static, no-body
definition; it is runtime-backed, so its placement is **server**: the loop is real
Elixir running on the BEAM in the trusted tier. The `agent` word names it; the nexus
binds the model behind the [[The Contract]] seam, so swapping models is config, never
a rewrite.

agent :analyst do
  model "minimax/minimax-m3"

  system """
  You are a B2B sales analyst. You draft the FIRST outreach email to a ranked lead.
  Rules:
  - One angle per lead. Pick it from the lead's shape, never generic.
  - Two sentences of body, one question to close. No pleasantries, no SKU dump.
  - Name the company. Reference its size or revenue when it sharpens the point.
  Use the tools to read leads and write drafts; stop when every top lead has a draft.
  """

  tools [
    AnalystTools.top_leads(),
    AnalystTools.context(),
    AnalystTools.draft()
  ]
end

The `system` string is the policy; the `tools` list is the capability surface. The
loop the nexus runs around this definition is the classic agent shape — propose,
act, observe, repeat:

defmodule Analyst do
  def run(limit \\ 3) do
    mem = %AnalystMemory{model: "minimax/minimax-m3"}

    AnalystTools.top_leads(limit)
    |> Enum.reduce(mem, fn %Lead{} = lead, acc ->
      draft = AnalystTools.draft(lead)
      AnalystMemory.remember(acc, draft)
    end)
  end

  def drafts(%AnalystMemory{drafts: drafts}), do: Enum.reverse(drafts)
end

## Why this runs on the server

The analyst needs the model key, it reads from the shared pipeline data, and its loop
must survive a browser refresh. All three say **server**: this is the trusted nexus
tier, native Elixir on the BEAM, not wasm in a tab. A client island like [[Chart]]
renders the *result* — the drafts — but the thinking happens where the secrets and the
state live. That split is the whole point of two placements: untrusted UI in the
browser, trusted reasoning on the nexus, one Dock between them.

The drafts the analyst produces become real work: each one becomes a `%Task{}` on the
[[Plan]] board with `status: :todo` and an `owner`. From a ranked number to a sentence
to a tracked job — the agent is the hinge.
